\fs36\b Phase 1 Implementation Report\b0\par
\fs24 Emergency Response and Personal Safety System for Kenya\par
Date: 2026-09-23\par
Prepared by: GitHub Copilot in VS Code\par\par

\b 1. Executive Summary\b0\par
Phase 1 of the Emergency Response and Personal Safety System has been implemented for the requested scope: environment setup, Firebase backend foundation, and registration/login for two public roles only: Resident and Responder. Responders are modeled as organizations or bodies, never as individual emergency workers. Hospitals are handled as responder organizations using organizationType = Hospital. No HospitalAdmin role, facilities collection, incidents, dispatch, trusted contacts, safety check-ins, or hospital-specific features were added in this phase.\par\par
The implementation prioritizes security by default, Kenya Data Protection Act consent handling, Firebase custom claims, Firestore security rules, and forensic audit logging through Cloud Functions. The work was committed to Git in commit 302204f with message: Implement Phase 1 Firebase auth setup.\par\par

\b 2. Work Completed\b0\par
\b 2.1 Environment Setup\b0\par
Flutter doctor was run before writing application code. The local toolchain reported no issues. Flutter dependencies were installed with flutter pub get, and Cloud Functions dependencies were installed with npm install. The Firebase project already had FlutterFire configuration for project emergencyresponsesystem-166601, and this configuration was used for backend validation.\par\par
The repository already had Git initialized with an earlier initial structure commit. After Phase 1 implementation, a new commit was created: 302204f, Implement Phase 1 Firebase auth setup. The working tree was clean after the commit.\par\par

\b 2.2 Firebase Backend Files\b0\par
The following backend files were added or updated:\par
- firebase.json: Configures Firestore rules, Firestore indexes, and Cloud Functions source.\par
- firestore.rules: Implements access control for users, responders, and auditLogs.\par
- firestore.indexes.json: Adds the Firestore indexes placeholder.\par
- functions/package.json and package-lock.json: Define the Node.js 20 Cloud Functions backend dependencies.\par
- functions/index.js: Implements the Cloud Functions triggers.\par
- functions/scripts/createFirstAdmin.js: Provides the one-off local SystemAdmin creation script.\par\par

\b 2.3 Firestore Schema Implemented\b0\par
The rules and Flutter code are aligned around the requested collections:\par
- users: Public registration creates Resident or Responder user documents with Firebase Auth UID as the document ID. The app records firstName, lastName, phoneNumber, email, role, preferredLanguage, dateOfBirth, gender, accountStatus, consentCrossBorderTransfer, createdAt, updatedAt, and lastLoginAt.\par
- responders: Responder registration creates an organization record using the same UID as the linked user document. It stores organizationName, organizationType, registrationNumber, verificationStatus, verificationNotes, verifiedBy, verifiedAt, availabilityStatus, currentLatitude, currentLongitude, serviceArea, createdAt, and updatedAt.\par
- auditLogs: Client writes are denied. Cloud Functions write audit entries through the Firebase Admin SDK.\par\par
No additional application collection was introduced for Phase 1. The auditLogs collection was implemented because it was explicitly required for digital forensics and Cloud Function write-only logging.\par\par

\b 2.4 Firestore Security Rules\b0\par
The rules enforce the following:\par
- users can be read and updated only by the document owner or a SystemAdmin custom claim.\par
- users can be created only by the authenticated owner for their own UID.\par
- public registration can create only Resident or Responder roles.\par
- role is immutable after user creation, preventing client-side role escalation.\par
- responder documents can be read by authenticated users.\par
- responder documents can be created only once by the owner for their own UID.\par
- responder owners may update only availabilityStatus, currentLatitude, and currentLongitude.\par
- responder organization identity fields are owner-writable only at creation and immutable afterward.\par
- SystemAdmin users may update only responder verification fields: verificationStatus, verificationNotes, verifiedBy, and verifiedAt.\par
- delete operations are denied for users, responders, and auditLogs.\par
- auditLogs are readable only by SystemAdmin custom claim and cannot be written by clients.\par\par

\b 2.5 Cloud Functions\b0\par
The following Cloud Functions were implemented:\par
- setUserRoleClaim: Runs when users/{userId} is created and sets the Firebase Auth custom claim role to the Firestore role value. It also writes a ROLE_CLAIM_SET audit log.\par
- logUserRegistration: Runs when users/{userId} is created and writes a USER_REGISTERED audit log entry.\par
- logResponderVerificationChange: Runs when responders/{responderId} is updated. It logs RESPONDER_VERIFIED, RESPONDER_REJECTED, or RESPONDER_SUSPENDED only when verificationStatus actually changes to the corresponding state. The previous verification state is captured.\par\par

\b 2.6 One-Off SystemAdmin Creation Script\b0\par
The file functions/scripts/createFirstAdmin.js was added. It is not deployed as a callable function and is not exposed to the Flutter app. It is intended to be run locally one time by the developer using Firebase Admin SDK credentials.\par\par
The script creates exactly one SystemAdmin account by checking both Firestore users documents and Firebase Auth custom claims before creating a new admin. It creates the Auth account, sets role = SystemAdmin as a custom claim, writes the users document, and writes a ROLE_CLAIM_SET audit log.\par\par

\b 2.7 Flutter Authentication and Registration\b0\par
The Flutter app now includes a registration screen and a sign-in screen. Registration captures fields in the requested order, with the requested role-specific responder fields shown only when the selected role is Responder. The phone number is validated against the Kenya E.164 format pattern for +2547XXXXXXXX or +2541XXXXXXXX. Passwords require at least 8 characters and must match the confirmation field. KDPA cross-border consent is required before account creation.\par\par
On successful registration, the app creates a Firebase Auth user, writes the users document, writes the responders document when the selected role is Responder, forces an ID token refresh, and then lets routing move the user to the correct screen.\par\par
Login supports email and password. Firebase Auth error codes are mapped to human-readable messages such as incorrect email or password, no account found, malformed email, disabled account, and too many requests. Raw Firebase error strings are not shown directly to the user.\par\par

\b 2.8 Role-Based Routing\b0\par
GoRouter was configured with role-aware redirect logic:\par
- Unauthenticated users go to /sign-in.\par
- Residents go to /home.\par
- Verified responders go to /responder-home.\par
- Pending responders go to /pending-verification.\par
- Rejected responders go to /registration-rejected.\par
- Suspended responders go to /account-suspended.\par
- SystemAdmin users go to /admin-dashboard.\par\par
Pending, rejected, and suspended responder screens are separate screens with distinct messages. The rejected screen can show verificationNotes when present and does not say the account is awaiting verification. The suspended screen also does not say the account is awaiting verification. The SystemAdmin dashboard is a placeholder for Phase 1 only.\par\par

\b 2.9 Mobile Permissions\b0\par
Android and iOS permission declarations were added for location and notifications. The Flutter app includes a PermissionService that requests notification permissions for Residents and notification plus location permissions for Responders on supported mobile platforms. This keeps the app ready for later emergency response workflows while keeping Phase 1 limited to authentication and setup.\par\par

\b 2.10 Documentation and Ignore Rules\b0\par
The root README was expanded with setup steps, backend deployment commands, createFirstAdmin usage, the KDPA cross-border consent rationale, and security notes. The .gitignore was updated to exclude serviceAccountKey.json, .env, build folders, and node_modules folders.\par\par

\b 3. Validation Performed\b0\par
The following checks were run:\par
- flutter doctor: completed successfully with no issues found.\par
- flutter pub get: completed successfully after adding permission_handler.\par
- flutter analyze: completed successfully with no issues found.\par
- firebase deploy --only firestore:rules --dry-run --project emergencyresponsesystem-166601: Firestore rules compiled successfully.\par
- node -e require(\"./index.js\"): Cloud Functions entrypoint loaded successfully.\par
- git status --short: clean after commit.\par\par

\b 4. Challenges Encountered\b0\par
\b 4.1 Firebase CLI Active Project\b0\par
The first Firestore rules validation attempt failed because Firebase CLI did not have an active project selected. This was resolved by rerunning the dry run with the explicit project ID emergencyresponsesystem-166601.\par\par
\b 4.2 Local Node.js Version Warning\b0\par
npm install showed an engine warning because the local machine is using Node.js 24 while the Cloud Functions backend is configured for Node.js 20. This does not stop deployment because Firebase Functions runtime is set to Node.js 20, but local development would be cleaner if Node.js 20 were used through nvm or another version manager.\par\par
\b 4.3 npm Audit Findings\b0\par
npm install reported moderate transitive dependency audit findings from the Firebase Functions dependency tree. They were not automatically forced because npm audit fix --force can introduce breaking package upgrades. These should be reviewed before production deployment.\par\par
\b 4.4 Word Document Generation\b0\par
An initial attempt to generate a true .docx file through PowerShell Open XML packaging failed because the available Compress-Archive behavior required a .zip output path. To keep the deliverable usable immediately, this report was produced as an RTF-based .doc file, which opens in Microsoft Word.\par\par
\b 4.5 End-to-End Firebase Testing Not Completed\b0\par
The app was not tested through live registration/login writes after deployment because the backend was not deployed and the one-off SystemAdmin credentials were not available. Static validation and rules compilation were completed instead.\par\par

\b 5. Pending Work\b0\par
The following items remain pending before production use:\par
- Deploy Firestore rules and Cloud Functions to Firebase.\par
- Run createFirstAdmin exactly once from a controlled local developer machine using a service account key and strong admin credentials.\par
- Test Resident registration end to end against live Firebase.\par
- Test Responder registration end to end against live Firebase.\par
- Confirm that Firebase custom claims are set and refreshed correctly after registration.\par
- Build the real SystemAdmin verification dashboard in a later phase. The current /admin-dashboard route is only a placeholder.\par
- Add Firebase Emulator Suite security rule tests for users, responders, and auditLogs.\par
- Review and address npm audit findings where compatible package updates are available.\par
- Add a production privacy policy, retention schedule, and operational data handling procedure for KDPA compliance.\par
- Configure Firebase App Check before production to reduce abuse from unauthorized clients.\par
- Configure monitoring, alerting, and log review procedures for Cloud Functions and audit events.\par
- Perform real iOS and Android device testing for location and notification permission flows.\par\par
The following items were intentionally not built because they are outside Phase 1:\par
- incidents.\par
- dispatch workflows.\par
- trusted contacts.\par
- safety check-ins.\par
- hospital-specific features.\par
- HospitalAdmin role.\par
- facilities collection.\par\par

\b 6. Recommendations\b0\par
- Deploy the backend before any live registration testing so Firestore rules and Cloud Functions are active.\par
- Run createFirstAdmin only once, locally, and never expose it through the app or as a callable function.\par
- Use Node.js 20 locally for Cloud Functions work to match the deployed Firebase runtime.\par
- Add automated Firestore rules tests before expanding the schema in Phase 2.\par
- Keep public registration limited to Resident and Responder. Any future SystemAdmin or internal role creation should remain server-controlled.\par
- Add App Check, monitoring, and alerting before production release.\par
- Prepare KDPA-facing legal documents before handling real user data, including privacy notice, consent wording review, data retention policy, and data subject request process.\par
- Expand SystemAdmin tools next so responder verification can be performed securely without manual Firestore edits.\par
- Continue using immutable audit logs for critical actions, especially verification decisions, role changes, and future incident/dispatch workflows.\par\par

